2.24.m:
Duty and Vedic knowledge of Brahman do not arise from anywhere else;
therefore, the twice-born should place faith in duty and the Purāṇa.

Thus ends the twenty-fourth chapter in the latter section of the compilation of six thousand verses in the glorious Kūrma Purāṇa.

2.25.m:
The householder should be known as two-fold: the practitioner and the non-practitioner.
Teaching, officiating at sacrifices, and receiving gifts are mentioned for the former;
he may carry out usury, agriculture, and commerce, [provided they are] not done by himself.

2.27.m:
He should not eat anything at night; at night he should be devoted to meditation,
his senses conquered, his anger conquered, contemplating the knowledge of reality;
he should always be celibate and should not consort even with his wife.

He may endure the five fires, or drink smoke, or consume warm food, or drink soma;
he should drink milk during the bright fortnight, and [consume] cow dung during the dark fortnight,
or eat fallen leaves, or sustain himself always with severe penances.

2.28.m:
He should simply await his time, as a servant awaits a command;
he should never study, nor speak, nor listen;
knowing thus, the supreme yogin is fit for becoming Brahman.

Or the wise man may wear a single garment, or wear only a loincloth;
he may be shaved-headed or wear a crest; he should carry the triple staff, possess nothing,
always wear ochre robes, and be intent on the yoga of meditation.

He should dwell at the edge of a village, at the root of a tree, or in a temple of the gods;
equal toward enemy and friend, and likewise toward honour and dishonour;
he should sustain himself always on alms, and never eat at one house only.
